<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bitlis</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bitlis is a city in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-van">Lake Van</a>, in a narrow valley on the route between the Armenian highlands and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-mesopotamia">Upper Mesopotamia</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was held by Arab, Kurdish and Turkish rulers, among them the Dilmaçoğulları, and from the 14th century it was the seat of the Kurdish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> of Bitlis. Its castle dominates the old town, and it was the home of the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharaf-khan-bidlisi">Sharaf Khan Bidlisi</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bitlis.jpg" alt="Bitlis" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: See above · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bitlis_Montage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Bitlis stands at an elevation of 1,545 metres in the steep-sided valley of the Bitlis River, a tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a>, 15 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-van">Lake Van</a>. The origin of its name is not known. To <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> it was Baghesh, one of the most important cities of the province of Aghdznik' in the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, and the main fortress of the canton of Salnodzor. The fortress guarded the Baghesh Pass, which linked the southern edge of the Armenian plateau with northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> the town was Balaleison. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> conquered it at the end of the seventh century, and it became capital of the Zurārid emirs of Arzan, prospering on an important trade route. Later it passed to the Shaybanid and Kaysite emirs, and the Byzantine general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-kourkouas">John Curcuas</a> captured it in the tenth century. Many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurds">Kurds</a> settled there, and the Marwanid dynasty took the city. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-manzikert">Manzikert</a> it came under Togan Arslan of Akhlat, after brief Dilmachoglu rule, and then Ayyubid rule, the Sultanate of Rûm and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a>.</p><p>Bitlis was a Kurdish emirate, often subordinate to larger powers but keeping a measure of independence; in the 14th century its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a>, the Rusaki family, were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the Karakoyunlu, and the emir submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> in 1394. In the 15th century the town flourished as a centre of Armenian manuscript production, the Kurdish emirs permitting monasteries to be built. An Ottoman army besieged it for three months and withdrew; in 1533 the emir Sheref was killed and his son submitted to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. The Ruzakid dynasty lasted until 1849, when an Ottoman governor evicted the last emir. Persian was the language of administration and scholarship, in the works of Idris-i Bidlisi and Sharaf Khan among others.</p><p>In 1915, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenian-genocide">Armenian genocide</a>, Turks and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurds">Kurds</a> led by Jevdet Bey massacred some 15,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> in Bitlis and its surroundings. Russian forces took the town in March 1916 and abandoned it in August.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Bitlis keeps more medieval and traditional architecture than any other town in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, much of it in the light-brown local stone called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahlat">Ahlat</a> stone. It has the 12th-century Ulu Mosque with a 15th-century minaret and large stone houses. Only one 19th-century Armenian church survives of the five monasteries and several churches that stood until 1915. Its population was 53,023 in 2021.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bitlis” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Known mainly from brief mentions in Arabic chronicles, the dynasty descended from a Turkish commander named Dilmac who received <b>Bitlis</b> and Erzen, south-west of Lake Van, after the Seljuk conquest of the region in the late 11th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/dilmacogullari">Dilmaçoğulları</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Bitlis</b>, at a gorge south-west of Lake Van, came under the Rojiki, a Kurdish tribal confederation, whose emirs claimed descent from ancient Kurdish lords.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-bitlis">Emirate of Bitlis</a></li></ul></section>
